#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3336334f-8a0b-5ca2-a99d-5178a4117375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+e\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723204} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9df6be-9ad0-50c0-88c8-133a3336e26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(2 + e\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723205} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5cbf15-ed6d-5e1e-acc7-a62c39190307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723208} {:answer/id #uuid "c34dcde6-4e12-5bcf-bd1a-a83fdda9fe8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(2 + e\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723216} {:answer/id #uuid "def25873-5df7-54f6-80ca-9d5b190e247f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 + e\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723223}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723223} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdfcaac-e574-51cc-bc60-e2ca6fc656f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53ae67a3-b161-552f-a5a7-7d6a8c1ef5ed" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=- x^{2} + x y + 2 e$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-2,e)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=x^{2} - x y + 2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=2 e$, and $f(-2,e)=2 e$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x - y + 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x + \\frac{2 x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-2,e)&=2 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(e\\right) + 2 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &=- e - 2\\\\ f_y(-2,e)&=- \\left(-2\\right) + \\frac{2 \\left(-2\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)}\\\\ &=2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-2,e)=\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 + e\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,e)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-2,e)&=\\left(- e - 2\\right)\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\left(- e - 2\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af0c0266-1bb7-560c-804b-a7d37c65b3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723214} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc6c642-532d-5e12-92b6-a281eda2208f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(3 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723217} {:answer/id #uuid "d2409191-3653-549f-9354-adec33c02eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723220} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dd754b-c815-583f-9895-5bbe0445d652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723227} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c695d6-41be-50b4-9260-d8715acc52d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723220} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe423df-2fbb-5976-b419-e61383f7f578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83694a46-8330-5a09-ab37-abedc02976a0" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x e^{y^{2} + 1}=x y - 3 y - 4 + e^{2}$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=- x y - x e^{y^{2} + 1} + 3 y$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=-4 + e^{2}$, and $f(-1,-1)=-4 + e^{2}$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- y - e^{y^{2} + 1}\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 2 x y e^{y^{2} + 1} - x + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-1,-1)&=- \\left(-1\\right) - e^{\\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 1}\\\\ &=1 - e^{2}\\\\ f_y(-1,-1)&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(-1\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 1} - \\left(-1\\right) + 3\\\\ &=4 - 2 e^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-1,-1)=\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives the listed tangent vector $-\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$. The gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-1,-1)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-1,-1)&=\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c136c3e-30c4-5ec3-ba80-14c5fb06d244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723200} {:answer/id #uuid "534ae40c-c8a4-5c6a-bdff-a3bf108d01b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,\\mathbf i+6\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723206} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b70d4-9208-517a-a91b-343635fc480f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\mathbf i+6\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723213} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a63099-e753-5e81-9dd4-fec764a2e2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723219} {:answer/id #uuid "f6526af3-7976-52a7-88ac-043d4417706a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723213} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e1554b-2b34-5573-826b-5ce64e509f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2489c47-3a37-5f61-b939-0568986e9678" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} y=y^{2} + 2$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} y - y^{2}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=2$, and $f(1,1)=2$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=6 x y\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=3 x^{2} - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(1,1)&=6 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\ &=6\\\\ f_y(1,1)&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right)\\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1)=\\mathbf i-6\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives the listed tangent vector $-\\mathbf i+6\\,\\mathbf j$. The gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\mathbf t(1,1)&=\\left(6\\right)\\left(1\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(1\\right)\\left(6\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038157ec-524b-5c84-b1df-6604a3389995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv^2+2uv+u^2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724151} {:answer/id #uuid "395e32a3-9249-5e72-925a-58b3223c680d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-uv^2+2uv-u^2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724153} {:answer/id #uuid "44f60925-32f6-5045-a041-83412542f79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv^2+u^2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724155} {:answer/id #uuid "d326e9b0-a7af-538e-a50b-5146ce6692cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv^2-2uv+u^2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724166} {:answer/id #uuid "f6de9c7c-43c1-54f1-9357-6af6d7deb8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv^2-uv+u^2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724166} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe4147a-c35f-5d5a-97e5-9b35eed64f36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3bb53a35-1a70-51eb-a2a4-b2d17b554287" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle xz,2y,xz\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,uv,v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. If\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You may use $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle v,-1,u\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For the stated orientation, the flux integrand is the dot product with the supplied cross product, $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=uv$, and $z=v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle uv,2uv,uv\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N&=(uv)v+(2uv)(-1)+(uv)u\\\\&=uv^2-2uv+u^2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D(uv^2-2uv+u^2v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e00336-ab60-5703-afe4-45930df78be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724152} {:answer/id #uuid "5dffae7f-50f0-544c-af59-aeb4989914cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724157} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf439d8-3b7a-57cd-959d-4fce004b1046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724162} {:answer/id #uuid "d762c9a0-43be-5709-80bd-7cd76414a488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724168} {:answer/id #uuid "dc070519-7884-5a6b-91a6-990fd72f4f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724162} :answer-field/id #uuid "616e04c0-77dd-5267-ac55-e8cf7b026acc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd318fc3-5c1f-56d3-89ec-953ac40f6407" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle xz,3y,1\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle uv,u+v,1-v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. If\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D(auv^2+bv^2+cuv-u+v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "For the stated orientation, use $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$ in\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nFirst compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u=\\langle v,1,0\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r_v=\\langle u,1,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTheir cross product is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\v&1&0\\\\u&1&-1\\end{vmatrix}=\\langle-1,v,v-u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=uv$, $y=u+v$, and $z=1-v$ gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle uv(1-v),3(u+v),1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow form and simplify the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N&=-uv(1-v)+3v(u+v)+(v-u)\\\\&=-uv+uv^2+3uv+3v^2+v-u\\\\&=uv^2+3v^2+2uv-u+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients with the given integrand, $a=1$, $b=3$, and $c=2$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+3+2=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48db3cb4-cc37-5735-91c0-efba15a6551e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv^2-u^2v+u^3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724156} {:answer/id #uuid "68f8c8da-31ef-5025-a0e1-0cd1c1e3aa42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2uv^2+u^2v-u^3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724159} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e1b4e-d3cf-5c8c-8474-a3a49d927e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv^2-u^2v-u^3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724160} {:answer/id #uuid "88a7e4e3-acc2-5016-a5b7-678ed4a8af13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv^2+u^2v+u^3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724161} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d598be-9f59-5034-8463-049983773759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv^2+u^3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724156} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb30dcb9-a650-5ee8-85f6-e180b851742d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d51f128c-d67d-56d7-abe2-68e831969ea3" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle z,y,xz\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,-u^2v,v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ so that its unit normal has a **positive $y$-component**. If\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. Begin with the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u=\\langle1,-2uv,0\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r_v=\\langle0,-u^2,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute their ordered cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&-2uv&0\\\\0&-u^2&1\\end{vmatrix}=\\langle-2uv,-1,-u^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nIts $y$-component is negative. Reverse the direction to obtain the oriented area vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u=-\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle2uv,1,u^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe flux is then expressed using\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=-u^2v$, and $z=v$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle v,-u^2v,uv\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTake the dot product with the correctly oriented area vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N&=v(2uv)+(-u^2v)(1)+(uv)u^2\\\\&=2uv^2-u^2v+u^3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D(2uv^2-u^2v+u^3v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$ without reversing it would give the negative of this integrand. The unit-normal length factor cancels against the surface area factor, so no extra norm is required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d4f089-4fdc-5a63-b694-5bc9ba897e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724172} {:answer/id #uuid "5524e5de-9bba-5ec0-a3f8-39f56df3db27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac2{\\sqrt3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724180} {:answer/id #uuid "95017e2f-4604-5607-9ac1-cbbc487ddcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724185} {:answer/id #uuid "a95b4cb8-a34d-5319-8bfd-415d36fdfeb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724186} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ea2e9e-c9a5-537d-8629-22b5e2046137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724186} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0276fa-cd5e-59d3-8103-cbd941684a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "270bdb17-5c8c-524c-98ce-1b5bad265919" :question/problem "Evaluate the flux\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\langle x,y,z\\rangle\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nthrough the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,v,1+u+v\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le u\\le2,\\quad0\\le v\\le1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v.$\n\n*Hint:* You may use $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle-1,-1,1\\rangle$ and the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For the specified orientation, the parametric flux formula is\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u,$ $y=v,$ and $z=1+u+v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle u,v,1+u+v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the supplied vector product, the flux integrand is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)=-u-v+(1+u+v)=1.\n$$\n\nThe two variable terms cancel, so integrate the constant over the parameter rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S&=\\int_0^2\\int_0^1 1\\,\\mathrm dv\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^2[v]_{0}^{1}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=[u]_0^2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product already incorporates the surface area scale. Do not normalize it or multiply by its magnitude again."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "451c6158-e3e4-52da-bb78-852fff62ecad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724177} {:answer/id #uuid "5228fc31-5814-5fce-8f89-1b46436d5b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724179} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c6812f-523f-545f-94b0-afc4f6f4abc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724190} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd42876-adea-54f5-9b48-97f6ca99014c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724191} {:answer/id #uuid "e539ae57-fa4d-5730-ab72-db7c3ae745a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724179} :answer-field/id #uuid "326f293c-1697-5c96-b421-efa0cdb2e3bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29322cba-e163-59de-a3d4-216effcffbc0" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\langle x,y,z+1\\rangle\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,v,uv\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le u\\le2,\\quad0\\le v\\le1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v.$\n\n*Hint: You may use $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle-v,-u,1\\rangle.$*" :question/worked-solution "For the specified orientation, use\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nThe vector product is supplied. First substitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle u,v,uv+1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTake the dot product, keeping both negative components of the normal vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)&=u(-v)+v(-u)+(uv+1)\\\\&=1-uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand varies across the parameter rectangle. Integrate it over the given bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S&=\\int_0^2\\int_0^1(1-uv)\\,\\mathrm dv\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^2\\left[v-\\frac{uv^2}{2}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^2\\left(1-\\frac u2\\right)\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\left[u-\\frac{u^2}{4}\\right]_0^2\\\\&=2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3884c714-63ab-5fa1-a4e9-91d761bbf8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724176} {:answer/id #uuid "5e702e24-78fe-5f33-b129-92370f1a2bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724181} {:answer/id #uuid "62f55b50-6276-512b-aeb1-0e9829cc432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724182} {:answer/id #uuid "ba521742-7bb0-57c0-8670-a5e37371a617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724187} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1c9070-d404-5006-8c75-c60f0467714d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724176} :answer-field/id #uuid "492aa741-dfd0-5cc6-931a-0ff3dff1e508" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "66b13640-78fb-56a2-b880-2307872a65e4" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\langle x,y,z+1\\rangle\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u+v,u-v,uv\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le u\\le1,\\qquad0\\le v\\le2.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v.$" :question/worked-solution "For the specified orientation, the flux is\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nCompute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u=\\langle1,1,v\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r_v=\\langle1,-1,u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTheir cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&1&v\\\\1&-1&u\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\langle u+v,-(u-v),-1-1\\rangle\\\\&=\\langle u+v,v-u,-2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u+v,$ $y=u-v,$ and $z=uv$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle u+v,u-v,uv+1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet $g(u,v)$ denote the flux integrand. Taking the dot product gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=(u+v)^2+(u-v)(v-u)-2(uv+1)\\\\&=(u+v)^2-(u-v)^2-2uv-2\\\\&=2uv-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate over the parameter rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^2(2uv-2)\\,\\mathrm dv\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^1[uv^2-2v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^1(4u-4)\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=[2u^2-4u]_0^1\\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative result is signed flux in the specified orientation. Reversing the cross-product order would reverse its sign."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24216a9f-94a2-591c-bcd9-96b5f670b7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724392} {:answer/id #uuid "24ffb1d7-e5f2-501c-bb0f-0067a9477164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724393} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba92840-db52-5f5e-9d70-94f94f95d029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724394} {:answer/id #uuid "32f3d046-cb61-5c23-816a-dcb92b5d19aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724398} {:answer/id #uuid "5891cfbc-d45a-561f-b68f-90a8f15d05d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724399} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb6de33-2680-5a97-a0c6-84bb674555f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ec0d3f2-8032-501d-92be-1d347ba9e77c" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=2x-1,&y&=2x-4,\\\\x&=2y+2,&x&=2y+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose a change of variables.** Rearrange each pair of boundaries to expose its shared expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y&=1,&2x-y&=4,\\\\x-2y&=2,&x-2y&=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $u=2x-y$ and $v=x-2y$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** They become $u=1$, $u=4$, $v=2$, and $v=6$, giving\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq4,\\ 2\\leq v\\leq6\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangular region.](images/bd/bde331a044869e44b1a53ffea146b9c99253678024dda3ce1987700fb69827d6.png)\n\n**Step 3: Find the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\\begin{vmatrix}2&-1\\\\1&-2\\end{vmatrix}=-4+1=-3.\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Find the area scale factor.** The determinant is nonzero. The inverse is $x=(2u-v)/3$, $y=(u-2v)/3$, so the transformation is one-to-one. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=-\\frac13,\\qquad \\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac13.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\frac13\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\int_2^6\\int_1^4\\frac13\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac13(4-1)(6-2)=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c945082-5aa7-55be-810a-b79427518e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724395} {:answer/id #uuid "92660804-9211-500c-bf4f-3f8419deab6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724403} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe9de2b-26be-5038-9661-4bee3ce1ad96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724405} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c13d90-cabb-5569-bd9c-a4a35a1aabc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724407} {:answer/id #uuid "e4381648-1a54-557b-a24f-cc035b9f410d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724405} :answer-field/id #uuid "098028f6-2a59-559d-936e-65d432bc8d46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8dafeedd-4831-5b1f-9db2-be7e3591d3b8" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y&=1,&x+y&=3,\\\\x-y&=0,&x-y&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose a change of variables.** The same expression occurs on each pair of parallel boundaries. Choose\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\qquad v=x-y.\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The four lines become $u=1$, $u=3$, $v=0$, and $v=2$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq3,\\ 0\\leq v\\leq2\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangular region.](images/2b/2bf7a1e04cdcecfb7bfe3074191421fb24fe35bb973d762fab6b18e9049221b2.png)\n\n**Step 3: Find the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\\begin{vmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{vmatrix}=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Find the area scale factor.** The inverse is $x=(u+v)/2$, $y=(u-v)/2$, so this is an invertible linear change of variables. Its inverse Jacobian is $-1/2$; area uses its absolute value, $1/2$.\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2\\int_1^3\\frac12\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac12(3-1)(2-0)=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2bd199-8ceb-5409-870a-469911233eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\ln2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724397} {:answer/id #uuid "66dd60ce-0f8d-53c4-8716-d06a42ba91f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\ln2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724400} {:answer/id #uuid "ac267d94-42a2-53d1-a96d-ccd0054f0cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac43\\ln7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724404} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7d25bd-0523-5908-9512-f5961418fb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac43\\ln2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724408} {:answer/id #uuid "f242d8a7-7146-5d7b-8a61-7493a35fba3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\ln2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724397} :answer-field/id #uuid "68eb3d74-72ca-5a88-b363-98c1a0560758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c06978d0-7156-502b-afe7-65c123b89b9b" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac3x,\\qquad y=\\frac7x,\\qquad x=y^2,\\qquad x=8y^2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose a change of variables.** Since $x,y>0$, the boundary equations can be written as\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\qquad xy=7,\\qquad \\frac{x}{y^2}=1,\\qquad \\frac{x}{y^2}=8.\n$$\n\nChoose $u=xy$ and $v=x/y^2$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The transformed region is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq7,\\ 1\\leq v\\leq8\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangular region.](images/4f/4f46dbee2037dfa93fdd4e020a25e3d1386ec78332f4e713ee69c51e32128b36.png)\n\nFor $u,v>0$, the unique positive inverse is $y=(u/v)^{1/3}$ and $x=u^{2/3}v^{1/3}$. It maps this whole rectangle onto the required first-quadrant region.\n\n**Step 3: Find the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}y&x\\\\1/y^2&-2x/y^3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2x}{y^2}-\\frac{x}{y^2}=-3v.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Find the area scale factor.** Since $1\\leq v\\leq8$, the Jacobian never vanishes. Taking the reciprocal and then its absolute value gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=-\\frac{1}{3v},\\qquad\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.** The scale factor varies with $v$; it cannot be pulled outside both integrals as a constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_1^8\\int_3^7\\frac{1}{3v}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac{7-3}{3}\\int_1^8\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac43(\\ln8-\\ln1)=4\\ln2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027e32a8-1324-5e57-8e6e-e0e06bc8f992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "182" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724412} {:answer/id #uuid "27629976-e869-5519-bbde-55b048c97e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724413} {:answer/id #uuid "6277c0b2-02b7-5534-947c-f3930a48cf09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{182}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724419} {:answer/id #uuid "915b878e-d6a7-5221-882a-a460e823b6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724427} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a48b5f-30fd-5a5f-a314-d631da023090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724427} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ca77c3-fb49-5622-bb74-7e63b121057d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "45b09566-3161-5dc2-b38c-fa5bfaba21c1" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^2y,\\qquad v=xy^2,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=\\frac1{x^2}, & y&=\\frac8{x^2},\\\\x&=\\frac1{y^2}, & x&=\\frac{27}{y^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map takes $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. Its inverse $T$ takes the rectangle $\\Delta$ back to $D$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\nThe two pairs of curves map to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1/x^2&\\Longrightarrow u=1,\\\\y=8/x^2&\\Longrightarrow u=8,\\\\x=1/y^2&\\Longrightarrow v=1,\\\\x=27/y^2&\\Longrightarrow v=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq8,\\quad1\\leq v\\leq27\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\n![The transformed rectangle](images/d6/d63b7db25c9091d3667e048dc8e149381b49a805c8b32aa73c0f183f94cb050b.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2xy&x^2\\\\y^2&2xy\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=4x^2y^2-x^2y^2=3x^2y^2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Find the inverse area factor.**\n\nThe area factor must be written entirely in $u,v$. Multiplying the two supplied expressions gives\n\n$$\nuv=x^3y^3=(xy)^3,\\qquad x^2y^2=(uv)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSince $x,y>0$, there is no sign ambiguity. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3(uv)^{2/3}}=\\frac13u^{-2/3}v^{-2/3}.\n$$\n\nFor completeness, $x^3=u^2/v$ and $y^3=v^2/u$ give unique positive $x,y$ throughout $\\Delta$. The Jacobian is nonzero there.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\frac13\\int_1^8\\int_1^{27}u^{-2/3}v^{-2/3}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\frac13\\left[3u^{1/3}\\right]_1^8\\left[3v^{1/3}\\right]_1^{27}\\\\&=\\frac13\\cdot3(2-1)\\cdot3(3-1)\\\\&=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fb38741-c0fc-52e8-9549-b335d1739fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724414} {:answer/id #uuid "30afb3b8-80fe-5283-a958-bafb5e56497b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln\\left(\\frac52\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724415} {:answer/id #uuid "31769dd3-7ca7-5fa9-982e-15e122d5d66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724416} {:answer/id #uuid "62c2acb2-903b-59bb-afbb-afe25d499903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\ln\\left(\\frac52\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724420} {:answer/id #uuid "74db1499-0ffd-591f-8656-7ba77a513c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac13\\ln\\left(\\frac52\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724415} :answer-field/id #uuid "801ab505-8764-5c87-a709-555de9910733" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7961f614-29b9-5d88-8b42-f5ae1a5ff9c4" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x^2}{y},\\qquad v=xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=\\frac{x^2}{2}, & y&=\\frac{x^2}{5},\\\\y&=\\frac1x, & y&=\\frac4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map takes $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. Its inverse $T$ takes the rectangle $\\Delta$ back to $D$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\nEach pair becomes a pair of constant-coordinate lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x^2/2&\\Longrightarrow u=2,\\\\y=x^2/5&\\Longrightarrow u=5,\\\\y=1/x&\\Longrightarrow v=1,\\\\y=4/x&\\Longrightarrow v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\leq u\\leq5,\\quad1\\leq v\\leq4\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\n![The transformed rectangle](images/ce/ce4fe8d57aa88a91d88c848f060885f8840391beb4c34012236843a6bf832e65.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2x/y&-x^2/y^2\\\\y&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\frac{2x^2}{y}+\\frac{x^2}{y}=\\frac{3x^2}{y}=3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Find the inverse area factor.**\n\nTake the reciprocal and express the area factor in the new coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\frac{1}{3u},\\qquad \\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nHere $u>0$. Also $x^3=uv$ and $y=v/x$ determine a unique positive pair $(x,y)$ for every point of the rectangle, so the change of variables is one-to-one and nonsingular.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_2^5\\int_1^4\\frac{1}{3u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\frac{4-1}{3}\\int_2^5\\frac1u\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\ln5-\\ln2=\\ln\\left(\\frac52\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d3cddee-ff30-50dc-b7c7-edcfa98c0be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724417} {:answer/id #uuid "630e54ec-da55-5856-ae90-c580ceb051a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724421} {:answer/id #uuid "65805310-74af-5848-bcf5-6a76aef4e00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724422} {:answer/id #uuid "c3151584-06e5-5aba-882a-51327b6d2f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724428} {:answer/id #uuid "ef348a17-1259-5d66-a0d6-6a67bcaab850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724430} :answer-field/id #uuid "f65dceb8-b8f4-520e-b753-73ed932fdb55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d8d0bf0-ab5e-5b19-9f3d-de09da30bec7" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\qquad v=y+x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=2x-1, & y&=2x+1,\\\\y&=2-x, & y&=4-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map takes $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. Its inverse $T$ takes the rectangle $\\Delta$ back to $D$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\nSubstitute the given expressions into each pair of boundary equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x-1&\\Longrightarrow u=-1,\\\\y=2x+1&\\Longrightarrow u=1,\\\\y=2-x&\\Longrightarrow v=2,\\\\y=4-x&\\Longrightarrow v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):-1\\leq u\\leq1,\\quad 2\\leq v\\leq4\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\n![The transformed rectangle](images/fd/fd928a6596ec40ce28899e4c31f8e37b58e642552e4e239e7db28a4986c57d5c.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\\begin{vmatrix}-2&1\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix}=-3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Find the inverse area factor.**\n\nThe determinant of the inverse is the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=-\\frac13,\\qquad \\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac13.\n$$\n\nThe affine map is one-to-one. In fact, $x=(v-u)/3$ and $y=(u+2v)/3$ are positive throughout this rectangle. The determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac13\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\frac13(1-(-1))(4-2)\\\\&=\\frac43.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062a16e8-ee4a-5b15-87cb-bf2f2f66d30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724577} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f6f5c63-d908-52c0-83bd-268705260fcd" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03df6326-cdfe-5b4b-afda-e0f06ba063e7" :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - y - z,-2,- x - y\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle 2,v^{2},u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n-1\\le u\\le 0,\\qquad 1\\le v\\le 3.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle - 2 v,0,0\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle - 2 v,0,0\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=2,\\quad y=v^{2},\\quad z=u - v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle - u - v^{2} + v,-2,- v^{2} - 2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(- u - v^{2} + v)(- 2 v) \\\\ &=2 u v+2 v^{3} \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2} \\\\ T_{2}&=(-2)(0) \\\\ &=0 \\\\ T_{3}&=(- v^{2} - 2)(0) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=2 u v+2 v^{3} \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=u v^{2}+\\frac{v^{4}}{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{2 v^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{1}^{3}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=1}^{v=3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=8 u+\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=4 u^{2}+\\frac{68 u}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{0}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=-1}^{u=0} \\\\ &=\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3913729-b2ee-5d71-8bfc-481381778c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724586} :answer-field/id #uuid "0077dd5a-d694-53d4-92a6-d5727b69025e" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "45873efe-bbbf-5f4e-83d6-a5580e3154ab" :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - x^{2},-4,6 z - 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,- v,3 u v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 3,\\qquad 0\\le v\\le 2.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle 3 v,- 3 u,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle 3 v,- 3 u,-1\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=u,\\quad y=- v,\\quad z=3 u v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle - u^{2},-4,18 u v - 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(- u^{2})(3 v) \\\\ &=- 3 u^{2} v \\\\ T_{2}&=(-4)(- 3 u) \\\\ &=12 u \\\\ T_{3}&=(18 u v - 1)(-1) \\\\ &=- 18 u v+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=- 3 u^{2} v- 18 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}+12 u+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=- \\frac{3 u^{2} v^{2}}{2}- 9 u v^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}+12 u v+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{0}^{2}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=- 6 u^{2}- 12 u \\\\ &\\quad {}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=- 2 u^{3}- 6 u^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}+2 u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{3}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\ &=-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e343801-a87a-5df7-ad33-47f9d4db633e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{232}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724581} :answer-field/id #uuid "c896c95b-a4bd-57ba-81b6-61d08b330e59" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4cfefabf-28b1-5b38-8fe7-91e9b4534555" :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 2 x,- 2 y,2 z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle - u + v,u v,u + v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 3,\\qquad 1\\le v\\le 2.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle - u + v,2,- u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle - u + v,2,- u - v\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=- u + v,\\quad y=u v,\\quad z=u + v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle 2 u - 2 v,- 2 u v,2 u + 2 v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(2 u - 2 v)(- u + v) \\\\ &=- 2 u^{2}+4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2} \\\\ T_{2}&=(- 2 u v)(2) \\\\ &=- 4 u v \\\\ T_{3}&=(2 u + 2 v)(- u - v) \\\\ &=- 2 u^{2}- 4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=- 4 u^{2}- 4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 4 v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=- 4 u^{2} v- 2 u v^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{4 v^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{1}^{2}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=- 4 u^{2}- 6 u \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=- \\frac{4 u^{3}}{3}- 3 u^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{28 u}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{3}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\ &=- \\frac{232}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c933b699-477d-5099-a14d-d5ab4decbd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724589} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59d550f-20b7-58ee-863f-bd2b0d7239d9" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8efdf63d-2c0d-5135-88f1-6280d918997e" :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle x,- y,z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle v,u v,u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 2,\\qquad 0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u=\\langle u,-1,v\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle u,-1,v\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=v,\\quad y=u v,\\quad z=u.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle v,- u v,u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(v)(u) \\\\ &=u v \\\\ T_{2}&=(- u v)(-1) \\\\ &=u v \\\\ T_{3}&=(u)(v) \\\\ &=u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=3 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=\\frac{3 u v^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{0}^{1}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\frac{3 u}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=\\frac{3 u^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{2}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=2} \\\\ &=\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8118109c-513a-53e0-ba0e-00090d79525c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724583} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee8d217-7535-5e0b-a1d7-858f85ebd99e" :answer-field/key "flux" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9e696f6-d7db-5301-b5a7-c7bc1a7d57a8" :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 2 x,- 2 y,- z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u v,u + v,- u + v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 3,\\qquad 0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u=\\langle -2,u + v,u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle -2,u + v,u - v\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=u v,\\quad y=u + v,\\quad z=- u + v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle - 2 u v,- 2 u - 2 v,u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(- 2 u v)(-2) \\\\ &=4 u v \\\\ T_{2}&=(- 2 u - 2 v)(u + v) \\\\ &=- 2 u^{2}- 4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2} \\\\ T_{3}&=(u - v)(u - v) \\\\ &=u^{2}- 2 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}+v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=- u^{2}- 2 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=- u^{2} v- u v^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{0}^{1}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=- u^{2}- u \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=- \\frac{u^{3}}{3}- \\frac{u^{2}}{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{u}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{3}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\ &=- \\frac{40}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133f4a86-1778-551c-a20d-d6c8d2029603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724602} {:answer/id #uuid "2557d2ba-0aa1-5c48-b602-49e5e78e1f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724606} {:answer/id #uuid "40c077a1-3000-5449-bd5c-a4445ab5d145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724611} {:answer/id #uuid "4be65eb7-b544-59f8-a930-b92e897d0d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724615} {:answer/id #uuid "fa99a56f-681f-517f-8a6b-cf14776e94cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724606} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cb66c6-5fb4-58f6-9295-a20e90d158d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18190587-a2d4-543d-abb0-cbf299787b32" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle - x^{2},\\,- 2 x y,\\,- 2 z\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u,\\,v,\\,2 u v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u v + b u^{2} v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,2 v\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,2 u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&0&2 v\\\\0&1&2 u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 2 v,\\,- 2 u,\\,1\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=v$, $z=2 u v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle - u^{2},\\,- 2 u v,\\,- 4 u v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(- u^{2}\\right)\\left(- 2 v\\right) \\\\  & =2 u^{2} v \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(- 2 u v\\right)\\left(- 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =4 u^{2} v \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(- 4 u v\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\  & =- 4 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =6 u^{2} v - 4 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(6 u^{2} v - 4 u v\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=-4$, $b=6$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b=(-4)+(6)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b42b9b6-730c-5717-adf1-155c2317657e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724601} {:answer/id #uuid "2933e1e8-6c71-5829-a9ec-e280b705b02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724607} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb49b08-5e21-5918-8a60-1f14c07736db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724609} {:answer/id #uuid "895791a3-fd21-5aad-860a-91afd49eba76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724619} {:answer/id #uuid "ee87bf15-6cbd-5e9f-8a45-334f7a0574f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724609} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6cd01b5-164b-5adc-812a-fa3beb96f56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46c39030-6ac7-5cd2-b5a5-cce5337a77e3" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle - x z,\\,3 y,\\,-1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u v,\\,u + v,\\,1 - v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u v^{2} + b v^{2} + c u v + u - v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle v,\\,1,\\,0\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle u,\\,1,\\,-1\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\v&1&0\\\\u&1&-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -1,\\,v,\\,- u + v\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u v$, $y=u + v$, $z=1 - v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle u v^{2} - u v,\\,3 u + 3 v,\\,-1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(u v^{2} - u v\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =- u v^{2} + u v \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(3 u + 3 v\\right)\\left(v\\right) \\\\  & =3 u v + 3 v^{2} \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(-1\\right)\\left(- u + v\\right) \\\\  & =u - v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =- u v^{2} + 4 u v + u + 3 v^{2} - v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(- u v^{2} + 4 u v + u + 3 v^{2} - v\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=-1$, $b=3$, $c=4$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+(3)+(4)=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00eb3f59-5971-5707-a51f-d7e1881644cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724600} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9ce33d-de3d-5354-899f-938035410830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724610} {:answer/id #uuid "6484a46b-e348-5b1c-8a18-4e7161453e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724617} {:answer/id #uuid "eef3c367-c6e5-5be4-8302-68a08c32ca41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724632} {:answer/id #uuid "feba80fe-4b6d-5816-a94b-f80728c8e383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724617} :answer-field/id #uuid "196a346c-089f-53d2-88f7-55bd46744354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "475f6749-4a66-5630-b697-a08408353456" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle - 3 z,\\,- x z,\\,- 2 y z\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u,\\,u^{2},\\,v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u v + b u^{2} v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,2 u,\\,0\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle 0,\\,0,\\,1\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&2 u&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2 u,\\,-1,\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=u^{2}$, $z=v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle - 3 v,\\,- u v,\\,- 2 u^{2} v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(- 3 v\\right)\\left(2 u\\right) \\\\  & =- 6 u v \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(- u v\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =u v \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(- 2 u^{2} v\\right)\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =- 5 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(- 5 u v\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=-5$, $b=0$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b=(-5)+(0)=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b6948d3-6bef-5d5b-902e-febe26767f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724620} {:answer/id #uuid "950efd0e-5856-5240-bcc8-39a5ab8fa537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724622} {:answer/id #uuid "b0294b3a-89b1-5166-9439-eee1bf32e5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724624} {:answer/id #uuid "b67865a0-9c96-58f7-bb9b-e83e15ecdc2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724625} {:answer/id #uuid "d11410ad-2cd0-52ab-bd14-f72048e65085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724624} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf31b2a-bfd9-5edc-94c2-6495c60875f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d9fea0a-f049-5048-8ff5-c2e53831dcfc" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle -3,\\,0,\\,-3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u + v,\\,u^{2} + v,\\,u^{3} + 3 v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u^{2} + b u + c)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,2 u,\\,3 u^{2}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,1,\\,3\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&2 u&3 u^{2}\\\\1&1&3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 3 u^{2} + 6 u,\\,3 u^{2} - 3,\\,1 - 2 u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u + v$, $y=u^{2} + v$, $z=u^{3} + 3 v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle -3,\\,0,\\,-3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(-3\\right)\\left(- 3 u^{2} + 6 u\\right) \\\\  & =9 u^{2} - 18 u \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(0\\right)\\left(3 u^{2} - 3\\right) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(-3\\right)\\left(1 - 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =6 u - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =9 u^{2} - 12 u - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(9 u^{2} - 12 u - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=9$, $b=-12$, $c=-3$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b+c=(9)+(-12)+(-3)=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17dd2dda-39a0-5cfc-bf8a-9a17c1ef8eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724603} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba5a78c-d3e2-5fc6-97aa-fec386fa0190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724608} {:answer/id #uuid "4be62086-3569-5210-99c3-aadef2351789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724614} {:answer/id #uuid "69a7164b-20b4-5c15-9e3a-896f3667c87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724618} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4ce6c6-c4e5-58c7-9730-77b815fb9c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724614} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c7c29d-d721-5193-83ff-4c6f41d670f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a71d2548-054d-5802-a280-660e4ae3dea8" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle x + \\frac{2}{z},\\,y + \\frac{3}{z},\\,z\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,u e^{- v},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a e^{v} + b e^{- v})\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle e^{v},\\,e^{- v},\\,1\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,- u e^{- v},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\e^{v}&e^{- v}&1\\\\u e^{v}&- u e^{- v}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle u e^{- v},\\,u e^{v},\\,- 2 u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u e^{v}$, $y=u e^{- v}$, $z=u$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle u e^{v} + \\frac{2}{u},\\,u e^{- v} + \\frac{3}{u},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(u e^{v} + \\frac{2}{u}\\right)\\left(u e^{- v}\\right) \\\\  & =u^{2} + 2 e^{- v} \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(u e^{- v} + \\frac{3}{u}\\right)\\left(u e^{v}\\right) \\\\  & =u^{2} + 3 e^{v} \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(u\\right)\\left(- 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =3 e^{v} + 2 e^{- v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(3 e^{v} + 2 e^{- v}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=3$, $b=2$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b=(3)+(2)=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112c6dd2-5fba-54fc-a23b-790f941ad522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724792} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3da6d5-aa67-5bcf-8b71-ba792390a2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724803} {:answer/id #uuid "9628d43a-067f-5a00-b9e2-edf4df5026f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 5 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724805} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8f44e4-7961-5c33-9813-16258330ca8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724808} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3dd197-3b0e-5128-a8b4-5c609d99fe3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 15 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 15 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724808} :answer-field/id #uuid "03fd600a-e2a4-509c-b71e-ad5c3e7460b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0eaa44c9-76a9-52db-b777-b35828834b46" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\qquad v=x y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{6}{x},\\qquad y=5 x^{2}, \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=2 x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=2. \\\\ y=5 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=1. \\\\ y=\\frac{6}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 1\\leq v\\leq 6\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/e8/e8f27760c6528ebae373087463b01217d46d0ae81bdc43cf3899a0d587f89606.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 y}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3 y} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{v}}{\\sqrt[3]{u}},\\qquad y=\\sqrt[3]{u} v^{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 2&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=- \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{2}^{5}\\left[v\\right]_{1}^{6} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})\\right)\\left(6-(1)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5793dddd-308b-515d-a5f9-148651a90beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724798} {:answer/id #uuid "76c4003a-0e89-50f0-b7b6-bafcd973d7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724800} {:answer/id #uuid "789272b3-e2f3-59d6-8799-75ef4da38347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724801} {:answer/id #uuid "793b3dd9-a951-5bc2-9594-046e2522d6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724802} {:answer/id #uuid "e06d277c-0b59-5f92-92fa-60485287ad74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724801} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d0b2c4-be1d-5c5b-9dca-2ed0b6f73437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2f17bda5-b4f9-5fb3-a8fc-779a5068454c" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y^{2}}{x},\\qquad v=x y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{6}{x},\\qquad x=\\frac{y^{2}}{5}, \\\\ x &=\\frac{y^{2}}{3},\\qquad y=\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{y^{2}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=3. \\\\ x=\\frac{y^{2}}{5}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=2. \\\\ y=\\frac{6}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 2\\leq v\\leq 6\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/e4/e4bff7ea00777fc6f37c45f20c6bef2428f41b1d3d2a5503a17e398c942dfba1.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} & \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 y^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x}{3 y^{2}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v^{\\frac{2}{3}}}{\\sqrt[3]{u}},\\qquad y=\\sqrt[3]{u} \\sqrt[3]{v}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 3&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)=- \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{3}^{5}\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{3}^{5}\\left[v\\right]_{2}^{6} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)})\\right)\\left(6-(2)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f3ac12-b908-53c7-b6a5-e08cfee6b9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724789} {:answer/id #uuid "27094282-1c57-5ec1-bf72-63b5deef169e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724793} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3daa9e-8480-53b1-9617-741979274236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724811} {:answer/id #uuid "ade1b6b1-ad41-54a4-b461-cb1e65aa3475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724812} {:answer/id #uuid "fedba3b8-720b-5dc2-9bed-6aabfde32b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724821} :answer-field/id #uuid "513b7e37-c8fa-59dc-98fb-7254b898de26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90a0c698-2335-5cbb-8a43-ea2d43a28731" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} y,\\qquad v=\\frac{1}{x y},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{x^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{5}{x^{2}}, \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{5 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=1. \\\\ y=\\frac{5}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=1. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{5 x}&\\Longrightarrow v=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 1\\leq v\\leq 5\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/8b/8b0859278a3e240d0782cb88b878cf75175f2c890f57e520599ea9842c26f8d8.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 x y & x^{2} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\nThe reciprocal simplifies using the supplied products:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(xy)^2}{x^2y}=\\frac{1}{v^2u}.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- y \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=u v,\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 1&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{1}\\right)=\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{1}^{5}\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=1\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{1}^{5}\\left[- \\frac{1}{v}\\right]_{1}^{5} \\\\ &=1\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(0)\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}-(-1)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96331156-316f-5f82-83bd-48d802416957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724806} {:answer/id #uuid "986d7c7c-3638-5130-bddc-30c9aefa4c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724807} {:answer/id #uuid "e445a3c8-8cfe-5940-acb6-562bd85e399c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724818} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0e3567-f52d-5d78-9fcd-88be749830d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724822} {:answer/id #uuid "ff83cb5a-6abb-5918-b97e-2084d6de41c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{15} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724823} :answer-field/id #uuid "3071fd3f-c515-5594-9d44-fd033825d13e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd488911-a395-5a7a-8c5c-5b96f9fae006" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} y,\\qquad v=\\frac{1}{x y},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{5}{x^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x}, \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{5 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{2}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=2. \\\\ y=\\frac{5}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{3 x}&\\Longrightarrow v=3. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{5 x}&\\Longrightarrow v=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 3\\leq v\\leq 5\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/87/872071d31b3820b265dbbccdd68d7ec9c171a01733707a67f9a4b735965657f8.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 x y & x^{2} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\nThe reciprocal simplifies using the supplied products:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(xy)^2}{x^2y}=\\frac{1}{v^2u}.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- y \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=u v,\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 2&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=- \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=1\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{2}^{5}\\left[- \\frac{1}{v}\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=1\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}-\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{15} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e38f024-2114-5d8d-8892-d34b52d3187b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724796} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcb7310-e5bd-5fe8-a732-89823aba81a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724809} {:answer/id #uuid "a950b089-300f-5e6b-ae0d-1f421fc08521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724810} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4f2e75-0371-53ad-971b-a3e87ea244ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724813} {:answer/id #uuid "f029e191-65e5-57b7-82a0-725a8c282dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724813} :answer-field/id #uuid "60e2ef74-f0b4-5f4d-96af-a9e686618776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df825f1d-68a3-59ef-8a4f-3d7f49546f83" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\qquad v=\\frac{y^{2}}{x},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=5 x^{2},\\qquad x=\\frac{y^{2}}{3}, \\\\ x &=\\frac{y^{2}}{5},\\qquad y=3 x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=3. \\\\ y=5 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ x=\\frac{y^{2}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow v=3. \\\\ x=\\frac{y^{2}}{5}&\\Longrightarrow v=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 3\\leq v\\leq 5\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/4a/4a6fa2bb18e4ea82f8cd2189490c1d59e2b3437259ffd1107bf96c79d5a05f2e.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ - \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} & \\frac{2 y}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 y^{2}}{x^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x^{4}}{3 y^{2}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3 u^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3 u^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{v}}{u^{\\frac{2}{3}}},\\qquad y=\\frac{v^{\\frac{2}{3}}}{\\sqrt[3]{u}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{3}^{5}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{3 u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left[- \\frac{1}{u}\\right]_{3}^{5}\\left[v\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}-\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\left(5-(3)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}]}
